const title = 'Best Foods for Skin: My Grocery List for Glowing Skin and a Healthy Gut';
const description = 'The best foods for skin by what they do: spinach and the gut-skin link, carotenoids, matcha and pumpkin seeds, vitamin C foods, and cruciferous vegetables. My grocery list, with real numbers and honest limits.';
const faq = [
  ['What are the best foods for skin?', 'I look for variety rather than one superfood: leafy greens, orange vegetables, cooked tomatoes, berries, nuts and seeds, vitamin C fruit and vegetables, and cruciferous vegetables, alongside enough protein and healthy fats.'],
  ['What foods help with collagen?', 'Your body makes collagen from amino acids, and vitamin C is a required co-factor in that process, so I pair protein with vitamin C foods such as kiwi, red bell peppers and citrus. Dark berries are rich in antioxidants that help protect existing collagen. Eating collagen itself is not the only route, and evidence for collagen supplements is mixed.'],
  ['Which foods are good for clear skin?', 'There is no guaranteed list. Zinc and magnesium (pumpkin seeds), omega-3s (walnuts, flaxseed, fish), green tea polyphenols and fibre-rich vegetables are all part of my routine, and a lower glycemic load may help acne-prone skin. See also my note on hidden skin triggers.'],
  ['Is kiwi better than orange for vitamin C?', 'Per 100 g, kiwi contains roughly 90 mg of vitamin C against about 50 mg in an orange, and red bell pepper roughly 130 mg. A whole orange is still a great source, and adult women need 75 mg a day according to the US National Institutes of Health, so both fit easily.'],
  ['Is matcha good for skin?', 'Matcha is rich in EGCG, a polyphenol studied for calming and oil-regulating effects, mostly in small trials. It contains caffeine, so I would keep it to a cup or two and not late in the evening. Tea can reduce iron absorption, so drink it between meals if your iron is low.'],
  ['Do carrots and sweet potatoes really help skin?', 'They provide beta-carotene, which the body converts into vitamin A, and vitamin A supports skin renewal. Very high beta-carotene intake can turn skin slightly orange, which is harmless and fades, but high-dose beta-carotene supplements are not recommended for smokers.'],
  ['Can food replace sunscreen?', 'No. Carotenoids and antioxidants from food may add a small amount of protection over time, but they do not replace SPF.'],
];
