<article class="art">
<section class="p art-top"><a class="up arrow" href="/notes/" style="color:#8A8A8A">Back to notes</a>
<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Notes · Glow foods · Gut and skin, part 5 · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Best Foods for Skin: My Grocery List for Glowing Skin</h1><p class="serif stand">Glow from within starts at the grocery store. This is part 5 of my skin barrier and gut health series: the foods I put in my basket, grouped by what they do for the skin, from UV protection and cell turnover to collagen, redness and digestion, with real numbers and honest limits.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">save this for your next grocery run</div></div>
<div class="ph art-hero"><span>Image 1 · Hero<br><em>Cover collage: vegetable aisle, green sheet mask with a carrot juice, jar of chopped kiwi and apple next to the pink blender</em></span></div></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>“What are the best foods for skin?” is a hard question to answer with one food, so I think about groceries in groups. Some foods support the gut, some help skin renew itself, some are full of the co-factors collagen needs, and some are simply calming. When my basket has a bit of each, I feel I have covered my bases.</p>
<p>This is general information from my own reading and routine, not medical advice. Food supports your skin; it does not replace sunscreen, skincare or medical treatment.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Leafy greens like spinach support the gut-skin connection.</li>
<li>Orange vegetables, cooked tomatoes and dark berries bring carotenoids and antioxidants that support the skin against everyday stress.</li>
<li>Matcha, pumpkin seeds, walnuts and flaxseeds are my calming, barrier-friendly picks.</li>
<li>Kiwi, red bell peppers and citrus give vitamin C, which the body needs to make collagen.</li>
<li>Broccoli sprouts, cruciferous vegetables, garlic and onions support digestion and the gut.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The gut-skin connection: why I start with greens</h2>
<p>I explained this in <a href="/notes/gut-health-and-skin-barrier/">part 1</a>. The short version of how your gut shows up on your face:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Direct line.</strong> Your gut and skin constantly communicate through the immune system.</li>
<li><strong>Inflammation.</strong> An unbalanced gut can go along with redness, breakouts and skin reactivity.</li>
<li><strong>Nutrient gate.</strong> Your skin can only use the nutrients your gut actually absorbs.</li>
<li><strong>Stress link.</strong> Gut stress may weaken your outer skin barrier.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is why a bag of spinach is the first thing I grab. It is an easy way to add fibre, folate and plant compounds to a smoothie, an omelette or a salad.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 2<br><em>Bag of baby spinach or a bowl of leafy greens</em></span></div>

<h2>Best foods for UV protection and cell turnover</h2>
<p>These foods are rich in carotenoids and fat-soluble antioxidants:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Food</th><th>What I like about it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Sweet potatoes and carrots</em></td><td>High in beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A. Vitamin A supports natural skin cell turnover. Adult women need about 700 mcg RAE a day.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Cooked tomatoes</em></td><td>Packed with lycopene, a carotenoid that is easier to absorb when tomatoes are cooked and eaten with oil.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Dark berries, pomegranate</em></td><td>Rich in ellagic acid and anthocyanins, plant antioxidants that help protect collagen fibres from environmental stress.</td></tr></table>
<p>Reviews of carotenoids and sun protection suggest that eating carotenoid-rich foods consistently for a few months may modestly increase the skin’s resistance to sunburn. The effect is small and takes time.</p>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">Important</div><p>Food-based antioxidants may add a little support against UV damage, but they do not replace sunscreen. SPF stays the first step of my daytime routine. Also, very high beta-carotene intake can tint the skin orange (harmless), and high-dose beta-carotene supplements are linked with higher lung cancer risk in smokers, so get it from food.</p></div>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 3<br><em>Carrots, oranges, apple and ginger on a board</em></span></div>

<h2>Best foods for redness and oil balance</h2>
<p>These are rich in polyphenols and barrier-friendly minerals:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Food</th><th>What I like about it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Matcha, green tea</em></td><td>High in EGCG, a polyphenol studied for calming and oil-regulating effects on skin, mostly in small trials and with topical or concentrated extracts.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Pumpkin seeds</em></td><td>A top plant source of zinc and magnesium, which support skin repair and a calmer response to breakouts.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Walnuts, flaxseeds</em></td><td>Provide plant-based ALA (an omega-3) that supports healthy cell membranes and moisture.</td></tr></table>
<p>Zinc and magnesium have limits worth knowing: the adult upper limit for zinc is 40 mg a day from all sources, and for magnesium from supplements 350 mg a day, according to the US National Institutes of Health. Seeds as food are not a concern, but I go deeper on supplements in <a href="/notes/foods-and-supplements-for-skin/">my food and supplements guide</a>. Tea can reduce iron absorption, so I drink it between meals.</p>
<div class="figrow"><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 4<br><em>Matcha sachet and whisk, desk with tablet</em></span></div><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 5<br><em>Pumpkin seeds, walnuts and flaxseeds in small bowls</em></span></div></div>

<h2>Best foods for collagen and brightening</h2>
<p>Collagen needs vitamin C as a co-factor, so I make sure it is on my list every week. Here is how some popular sources compare, using approximate USDA values per 100 g:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Food</th><th>Vitamin C (about, per 100 g)</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Red bell pepper</em></td><td>around 130 mg</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Kiwi</em></td><td>around 90 mg</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Orange</em></td><td>around 50 mg</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Strawberries</em></td><td>around 60 mg</td></tr></table>
<p>Adult women need 75 mg a day (more for smokers), so one kiwi or half a red pepper already covers a large share. Heat and long storage reduce vitamin C, so I like these raw or lightly cooked. Citrus peel and lemon water also bring bioflavonoids, plant compounds that support small blood vessels, though there is little direct evidence for redness.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 6<br><em>Kiwi, limes, banana and the blender on the counter</em></span></div>

<h2>Best foods for hormonal and digestive support</h2>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Food</th><th>What I like about it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Broccoli sprouts, cruciferous vegetables</em></td><td>Contain sulforaphane and DIM, compounds studied for their role in the liver’s normal processing of hormones and in the skin’s own defence systems. Skin studies are small, often with extracts, so I eat them for general health.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Garlic, onions</em></td><td>Rich in prebiotic fibres and sulfur compounds that feed beneficial gut bacteria and supply building blocks for glutathione, one of the body’s main antioxidants.</td></tr></table>
<p>I do not believe in “detoxing” with food. Your liver and gut already do that job, and I simply like to give them good building blocks. If garlic, onions or cruciferous vegetables upset your digestion, start small, because they can cause gas and bloating.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 7<br><em>Plate with avocado toast, salmon, egg and berries; a glass of water with berries</em></span></div>

<h2>How I shop and eat them</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Frozen counts.</strong> Frozen berries and vegetables are picked ripe and are a good, cheap option.</li>
<li><strong>Add fat to carotenoids.</strong> Cook tomatoes and carrots with olive oil, since carotenoids are fat-soluble.</li>
<li><strong>Eat a colour a day.</strong> Orange, red, green and purple foods bring different compounds.</li>
<li><strong>Buy what you will actually eat.</strong> A basket you finish beats a perfect one that spoils.</li>
</ul>

<h2>My grocery list</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Baby spinach or other leafy greens</li>
<li>Carrots and sweet potatoes</li>
<li>Tomatoes, to cook with olive oil</li>
<li>Blueberries and other dark berries, pomegranate when in season</li>
<li>Matcha or green tea</li>
<li>Pumpkin seeds, walnuts and flaxseeds</li>
<li>Kiwi, red bell peppers, lemons and limes</li>
<li>Broccoli, sprouts, cauliflower, garlic and onions</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Glow from within is built in the grocery aisle, one basket at a time.</blockquote>

<div class="callout"><div class="up">A note</div><p>If you have allergies, take medication or have a medical condition, please check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before changing your diet, especially with large amounts of green tea, matcha or supplements. Grapefruit, for example, can interact with some medications. I am not a medical professional.</p></div>

